    "plan_strategy": "Switch family from the linear baseline to a gradient-boosted tree ensemble (hist_gbdt) to raise the ranking itself, since the bar sits above the baseline ranking's ceiling. Spambase is a dense, all-numeric, heavily skewed count/frequency matrix where axis-aligned boosted splits are known to order rows far better than a linear logit — published validation accuracies for boosted trees on spambase sit around 0.95-0.96 versus ~0.93 for logistic regression, so the required ranking lift (KS +0.0012) is well inside the expected family-swap effect. Fit one HistGradientBoostingClassifier with a moderate learning rate and a substantial iteration budget, using its own internal early stopping so the iteration count is chosen without overfitting the training split. Keep preprocessing minimal and honest: median imputation (no missingness exists in this file, so the imputer is a no-op) and no scaling, which trees do not need. Leave the operating point at the estimator's default rule — the classes are only 1.54:1 imbalanced, and balanced_accuracy on the baseline already sits within 0.019 of its own best-cut value, so the payoff is in the ranking, not in reweighting. Read cut_headroom, roc_auc, balanced_accuracy_at_best_cut and train_val_gap from this attempt to decide whether attempt 2 should add capacity (more leaves / lower learning rate / more iterations), add regularisation, or move the operating point."
